# 🔍 Grad-CAM: Explainable AI Using ResNet50

## 📌 Project Overview
This project explores Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) using Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM).

The objective is to visualize which regions of an input image contribute to a Convolutional Neural Network's classification prediction.

A pretrained ResNet50 model is implemented using PyTorch to perform image classification and generate visual explanations through Grad-CAM heatmaps.

## 🎯 Objectives
- Understand CNN-based image classification.
- Implement Grad-CAM visualization.
- Extract feature activations and gradients.
- Generate class activation heatmaps.
- Improve interpretability of deep learning predictions.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used
- Python
- PyTorch
- Torchvision
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Google Colab
- ResNet50
- Grad-CAM

## ⚙️ Methodology
1. Load a pretrained ResNet50 model.
2. Upload and preprocess an input image.
3. Perform image classification.
4. Extract convolutional layer activations.
5. Calculate gradients for the predicted class.
6. Generate the Grad-CAM heatmap.
7. Overlay the heatmap on the original image.

## 📊 Results
The experiment generates:
- Image classification predictions.
- Grad-CAM heatmaps.
- Visual overlays highlighting image regions associated with the selected prediction.

## 🚀 How to Run
1. Download the `.ipynb` notebook from this repository.
2. Open [Google Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/).
3. Upload the notebook.
4. Run the cells sequentially.
5. Upload an image when prompted.
6. View the prediction and Grad-CAM visualization.

## 📚 Key Learning Outcomes
- Practical understanding of CNN architectures.
- Gradient-based model interpretation.
- Feature map visualization.
- Hands-on experience with Explainable AI.

## 🔮 Future Improvements
- Experiment with different CNN architectures.
- Compare Grad-CAM with Grad-CAM++.
- Test on multiple image datasets.
- Evaluate explanations across different predicted classes.

## 👩‍💻 Project Category
Deep Learning | Computer Vision | Explainable AI

**Note:** This is an educational implementation demonstrating gradient-based visual explanations, not a complete evaluation of model interpretability.
